Lab 17: Scaling Pipelines with Parallel Tasks
Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Understand the concepts of parallel task execution in CI/CD pipelines
Configure Tekton pipelines to run multiple tasks simultaneously
Implement conditional logic to control when parallel tasks execute
Validate and monitor parallel task execution
Measure and compare pipeline performance to quantify the benefits of parallelization
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of Kubernetes concepts (pods, services, deployments)
Familiarity with YAML syntax and structure
Previous experience with Tekton Pipelines
Knowledge of Git version control basics
Basic command-line interface skills
This lab assumes access to an OpenShift or Kubernetes cluster with Tekton Pipelines already installed, and that the oc (or kubectl) and tkn CLI tools are available on your workstation. If tkn is not installed, install it with:

# Linux example (adjust for your OS/architecture as needed)
curl -LO https://github.com/tektoncd/cli/releases/latest/download/tkn_Linux_x86_64.tar.gz
tar -xzf tkn_Linux_x86_64.tar.gz tkn
sudo mv tkn /usr/local/bin/
tkn version
All work in this lab is performed in an isolated, self-owned practice namespace and is intended purely for learning how parallel CI/CD execution works and how to validate it safely.

Task 1: Understanding Parallel Task Execution
Subtask 1.1: Review Current Pipeline Structure and Create a Sequential Baseline
First, we build a basic sequential pipeline to serve as our performance baseline.

Verify your cluster connection:
oc whoami
oc project
Create a new project for this lab:
oc new-project pipeline-parallel-lab
Create the custom task definitions used by our pipelines. Each task simulates a real pipeline stage using sleep to represent processing time:
cat > code-quality-task.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1beta1
kind: Task
metadata:
  name: code-quality-check
  namespace: pipeline-parallel-lab
spec:
  workspaces:
    - name: source
      description: Source code workspace
  steps:
    - name: quality-check
      image: registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/ubi-minimal:latest
      workingDir: $(workspaces.source.path)
      script: |
        #!/bin/sh
        echo "Running code quality analysis..."
        sleep 10
        echo "Code quality check completed successfully"
        echo "Quality Score: 85/100"
EOF
cat > security-scanner-task.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1beta1
kind: Task
metadata:
  name: security-scanner
  namespace: pipeline-parallel-lab
spec:
  workspaces:
    - name: source
      description: Source code workspace
  steps:
    - name: security-scan
      image: registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/ubi-minimal:latest
      workingDir: $(workspaces.source.path)
      script: |
        #!/bin/sh
        echo "Running security vulnerability scan..."
        sleep 15
        echo "Security scan completed"
        echo "No critical vulnerabilities found"
EOF
cat > test-runner-task.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1beta1
kind: Task
metadata:
  name: run-tests
  namespace: pipeline-parallel-lab
spec:
  workspaces:
    - name: source
      description: Source code workspace
  steps:
    - name: unit-tests
      image: registry.access.redhat.com/ubi8/ubi-minimal:latest
      workingDir: $(workspaces.source.path)
      script: |
        #!/bin/sh
        echo "Running unit tests..."
        sleep 12
        echo "All tests passed successfully"
        echo "Test Coverage: 92%"
EOF
Apply all task definitions and verify they exist before proceeding:
oc apply -f code-quality-task.yaml
oc apply -f security-scanner-task.yaml
oc apply -f test-runner-task.yaml
Confirm all three custom tasks were created successfully:

oc get task -n pipeline-parallel-lab
tkn task list -n pipeline-parallel-lab
You should see code-quality-check, security-scanner, and run-tests listed by both commands. Do not proceed until all three tasks appear.

Create the sequential pipeline, which runs code analysis, security scanning, and tests one after another using runAfter:
cat > sequential-pipeline.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1beta1
kind: Pipeline
metadata:
  name: sequential-build-pipeline
  namespace: pipeline-parallel-lab
spec:
  params:
    - name: git-url
      type: string
      description: Git repository URL
    - name: git-revision
      type: string
      default: main
      description: Git revision to build
  workspaces:
    - name: shared-data
      description: Shared workspace for pipeline tasks
  tasks:
    - name: fetch-source
      taskRef:
        name: git-clone
        kind: ClusterTask
      workspaces:
        - name: output
          workspace: shared-data
      params:
        - name: url
          value: $(params.git-url)
        - name: revision
          value: $(params.git-revision)

    - name: code-analysis
      taskRef:
        name: code-quality-check
      workspaces:
        - name: source
          workspace: shared-data
      runAfter:
        - fetch-source

    - name: security-scan
      taskRef:
        name: security-scanner
      workspaces:
        - name: source
          workspace: shared-data
      runAfter:
        - code-analysis

    - name: unit-tests
      taskRef:
        name: run-tests
      workspaces:
        - name: source
          workspace: shared-data
      runAfter:
        - security-scan
EOF
Apply it:

oc apply -f sequential-pipeline.yaml
Deliverable: oc get task -n pipeline-parallel-lab lists all three custom tasks, and oc get pipeline sequential-build-pipeline -n pipeline-parallel-lab shows the pipeline created successfully.

Subtask 1.2: Run the Sequential Pipeline and Record Baseline Time
Create and run a PipelineRun:
cat > sequential-pipelinerun.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1beta1
kind: PipelineRun
metadata:
  name: sequential-run-1
  namespace: pipeline-parallel-lab
spec:
  pipelineRef:
    name: sequential-build-pipeline
  params:
    - name: git-url
      value: "https://github.com/openshift/ruby-hello-world.git"
    - name: git-revision
      value: "main"
  workspaces:
    - name: shared-data
      volumeClaimTemplate:
        spec:
          accessModes:
            - ReadWriteOnce
          resources:
            requests:
              storage: 1Gi
EOF
oc apply -f sequential-pipelinerun.yaml
Wait for completion and inspect the result:
tkn pipelinerun logs sequential-run-1 -f
tkn pipelinerun describe sequential-run-1
Record the timing:
oc get pipelinerun sequential-run-1 -o jsonpath='{.status.startTime}{"\n"}{.status.completionTime}{"\n"}'
Deliverable: The sequential pipeline reaches Succeeded status. Because code-analysis (10s), security-scan (15s), and unit-tests (12s) run one after another, total task time is roughly 37 seconds plus the git-clone step â you should observe a completion time noticeably longer than the sum of any single task, confirming strictly sequential execution.

Task 2: Implementing Parallel Task Execution with Conditional Logic
Subtask 2.1: Create a Parallel Pipeline
Now we modify the pipeline so that code-analysis, security-scan, and unit-tests all run immediately after fetch-source instead of waiting on each other, and we add when expressions to make security scanning and quality checks optional.

cat > parallel-pipeline.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1beta1
kind: Pipeline
metadata:
  name: parallel-build-pipeline
  namespace: pipeline-parallel-lab
spec:
  params:
    - name: git-url
      type: string
      description: Git repository URL
    - name: git-revision
      type: string
      default: main
      description: Git revision to build
    - name: enable-security-scan
      type: string
      default: "true"
      description: Enable or disable security scanning
    - name: enable-quality-check
      type: string
      default: "true"
      description: Enable or disable code quality check
  workspaces:
    - name: shared-data
      description: Shared workspace for pipeline tasks
  tasks:
    - name: fetch-source
      taskRef:
        name: git-clone
        kind: ClusterTask
      workspaces:
        - name: output
          workspace: shared-data
      params:
        - name: url
          value: $(params.git-url)
        - name: revision
          value: $(params.git-revision)

    - name: code-analysis
      taskRef:
        name: code-quality-check
      workspaces:
        - name: source
          workspace: shared-data
      runAfter:
        - fetch-source
      when:
        - input: "$(params.enable-quality-check)"
          operator: in
          values: ["true"]

    - name: security-scan
      taskRef:
        name: security-scanner
      workspaces:
        - name: source
          workspace: shared-data
      runAfter:
        - fetch-source
      when:
        - input: "$(params.enable-security-scan)"
          operator: in
          values: ["true"]

    - name: unit-tests
      taskRef:
        name: run-tests
      workspaces:
        - name: source
          workspace: shared-data
      runAfter:
        - fetch-source
EOF
oc apply -f parallel-pipeline.yaml
Confirm the pipeline is registered:

tkn pipeline list -n pipeline-parallel-lab
Deliverable: parallel-build-pipeline appears in the tkn pipeline list output.

